        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Old English</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 450 CE – c. 1150 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Family</span><span class="info-stat-val">West Germanic, from the Anglo-Frisian or Ingvaeonic (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> Germanic) dialects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>; its closest relatives are Old Frisian and Old Saxon</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Writing</span><span class="info-stat-val">runes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> futhorc (5th–7th centuries); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, introduced in the 7th century by Irish missionaries and supplanting the runes from around the 8th, in half-uncial and then Insular script, with the added letters æ, ð, þ and ƿ</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Earliest records</span><span class="info-stat-val">a small corpus of runic inscriptions of the 5th to 7th centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedmon">Cædmon</a>’s Hymn, composed between 658 and 680 and written down in the early 8th century; the runic texts of the Franks Casket, early 8th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Status today</span><span class="info-stat-val">extinct; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066 it developed into Middle English and so into Modern English, while its Northumbrian dialect became the source of Scots</span></div>
        </div>
