    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Old English grew from the dialects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a> who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 5th century, and it replaced the Common Brittonic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of Roman Britain over most of what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</li><li>It was a heavily inflected language, with five cases, three genders and a freer word order than Modern English; perhaps around 85% of its words are no longer in use, but those that survived are the basic elements of English vocabulary.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> standard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Æthelwold, it became a written language of government and literature, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066 made Anglo-Norman French the language of the upper classes.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>With few and relatively unimportant exceptions, all surviving Old English poetry is preserved in just four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>; the earliest English poet known by name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedmon">Cædmon</a>, was a lay brother at the monastery of Whitby.</p></aside>
    </section>

